Some field observations have no structured form field—why a site was chosen, how
the forest looked, who fell ill, or what puzzled the collector. NAHPU stores
this context in **narratives**: dated journal entries linked to a site and a
writer.

Heru's 2014 journal is rich in this kind of detail. It was also written by
someone who failed two standardized English tests that year. Before entering it
into NAHPU, clean up the English without changing a single fact.

## Rules for cleaning up a field journal

- **Keep every fact.** Leave counts, times, places, names, and observations as
  written, even when they seem minor.
- **Fix the language.** Correct grammar, tense, plurals, and word choice, and
  break up run-on sentences.
- **Fix scientific names transparently.** Correct misspelled or outdated names
  and keep the original in parentheses or brackets the first time it appears.
- **Preserve conflicts instead of editing them away.** When the journal and a
  specimen sheet disagree, enter both as written and explain the discrepancy in
  the specimen record (as in [Basic tutorial 5](/en/tutorials/basic/specimens/)).
- **Attach the scan.** Keep a scan or photo of the original handwritten page
  attached to the entry so anyone can verify your transcription.

## 1. Compare an original and corrected entry

Below is the 12 September 2014 entry from Heru's journal, with teammates' real
names redacted:

![Handwritten journal entry for 12 September 2014 with the teammates' names blacked out](../../../../../assets/images/tutorials/basic/journal-2014-09-12.jpg)

Transcribed as written (with fictional teammate names substituted):

```text
Bima, Citra, the guide, and I set 10 pitfalls around 50 meters from the camp.
then I set 31 snap traps next to the pitfalls. Both pitfalls and snap traps
called Line 1. Citra and Bima set 25 snap traps in other side called Line 2.
I got 1 squirrels right after I set the traps. Forest type almost similar all
line, which is slightly disturbed caused by hiker activities.
```

Cleaned up for NAHPU:

```text
Bima, Citra, Eko (our guide), and I set 10 pitfall traps about 50 m from the
camp. I then set 31 snap traps next to the pitfalls; together they form
Line 1. Citra and Bima set 25 snap traps on the other side of the camp as
Line 2. I caught a squirrel right after setting the traps. The forest is
similar along all the lines and slightly disturbed by hikers.
```

Every number, place, and observation stays intact. The squirrel is HH 436,
caught at 16:00 the same day.

## 2. Enter the journal entries

{% steps %}

1. Open `Narrative` and select `+`.
2. Enter the `Date`, select `Heru Handika` as `Writer`, and select `TJ-CAMP1`
   as `Site ID`.
3. Paste the corrected text. Narratives support Markdown, so wrap scientific
   names in underscores to italicize them (for example,
   `_Maxomys hylomyoides_`).
4. Attach the scanned journal page as media.
5. Select `Preview` to check how the entry renders.

{% /steps %}

Link all five entries to the camp (`TJ-CAMP1`). A narrative's site is where the
entry was written—not every location it mentions—and Heru wrote each entry at
the Pondok Merah camp, even on days spent at Pasir Putih. One entry can cover
several lines because each line has its own site record and is named in the
text. Switch the narrative's site only when an entry is written somewhere else,
such as a fly camp away from base camp.

| Date | Summary |
| --- | --- |
| 12 Sep 2014 | Lines 1 and 2 set |
| 13 Sep 2014 | First trap check at Pondok Merah |
| 16 Sep 2014 | Boat trip to Pasir Putih; Lines 3–5 set |
| 17 Sep 2014 | First trap check at Pasir Putih |
| 18 Sep 2014 | Lines pulled; the possible new *Maxomys* |

Below is the 18 September entry in preview mode, linked to `TJ-CAMP1`. `Time`
stays empty because the journal does not record when the entry was written:

![NAHPU Narrative page on an iPad for 18 September 2014, site TJ-CAMP1, writer Heru Handika, with the corrected entry rendered and Maxomys hylomyoides in italics](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/narrative-2014-09-18.webp)

## 3. Corrected entries to paste

### 13 September 2014

```markdown
Line 1 produced 14 rats and 6 shrews; all the shrews came from the pitfalls.
Line 2 produced 25 rats. Some traps caught more than one animal in a night,
because we rebaited them after each capture. The night temperature fell from
about 16 °C to 14 °C. There was no rain; the sky was sometimes cloudy and
sometimes very clear.
```

The Line 1 rat count is overwritten on paper and reads as 14. The weather notes
also appear in the `TJHH-L1` event's environmental data, where they can be
searched and exported.

### 16 September 2014

```markdown
Line 1 produced 3 shrews and 1 rat; Line 2 produced nothing. We hired a
fisherman, Fajar, to take us across the lake by wooden boat. Eko (our guide),
Fajar, Bima, Dian, and I took a 1.5-hour boat ride from the camp to a site
called Pasir Putih.

The new site was nearly pristine, with very little sign of human activity,
unlike the forest near the camp, which hikers have slightly disturbed. On the
way we counted five fishermen's shelters at three places, all beside the lake.

Bima and I set 20 snap traps at the first suitable place we found in Pasir
Putih. The forest there is nearly pristine. I saw only one trail, and it seemed
rarely used. There are many big trees for a montane forest, 1–2 m in diameter,
and most are covered in moss. The ground is fairly open, with few small plants.
_Elephantopus_ (an invasive Asteraceae) along the lake edge hides the forest
behind it.

We moved to a second place close to the first and set pitfalls. The forest here
is more open and the ground cover denser, but it looks intact. We think natural
processes opened the canopy; there are also fewer big trees than at the first
place.

At the last place we set 20 more snap traps, next to the pitfalls. This forest
differs from the other two: open in some areas and closed in others, with many
lianas. Bima and I set many of the traps on lianas. The moss cover on the trees
is similar to the first place. Because the three places are well separated and
have different forest conditions, I named the first trap line Line 3, the
pitfall line Line 4, and the last line Line 5.

While setting the traps, I heard siamangs (_Symphalangus syndactylus_; written
in the field as _Hylobates syndactylus_) close by. We also found edelweiss near
the site. We left camp at 09:45 and returned at 16:30.

I felt sick when we got back and had to lie down before preparing the
specimens. Overall, the team was still happy and enjoying camp life after five
nights at high elevation. One thing I don't understand is why we caught so few
birds compared with mammals, even compared with the average of the surveys we
have done so far.

Bima and Citra set most of the Line 2 snap traps above the ground, about
1–2 m high.
```

The habitat descriptions for the three Pasir Putih lines are also summarized on
`TJHH-L3`, `TJHH-P4`, and `TJHH-L5` (Tutorial 3). The journal preserves the
full story while the sites carry the structured details each specimen needs.

### 17 September 2014

```markdown
Line 2 produced 2 _Chiropodomys_, 2 _Niviventer_, and 1 _Maxomys_. Bima,
Citra, Eko, Dian, and Fajar checked and rebaited the Pasir Putih traps by boat,
while I prepared some of the rats in camp. They left at 08:00 and returned at
13:15 with 5 rats and 1 gymnure from Line 3, 6 shrews and 1 young gymnure from
Line 4, and 4 rats from Line 5.
```

This entry explains why `HH523` and `HH529` list `Bima Sampel` as collector in
Tutorial 5: Heru stayed in camp while the boat crew checked the traps. Two
counts are overwritten on paper and read as 1 gymnure and 6 shrews.

### 18 September 2014

```markdown
Line 2 produced 1 squirrel and 1 rat; Line 3, 5 rats and 2 gymnures; Line 4,
4 shrews; and Line 5, 3 rats. Citra, Dian, Eko, Fajar, and I pulled all the
pitfalls and snap traps from Lines 3, 4, and 5, and the mist net as well.

The survey at this new site, far from human access, added one shrew species
and what may be a new rat. The rat looks like _Maxomys hylomyoides_, but its
underparts are darker: more orange at the base and grey on the belly. We had
only one line set up.
```

This entry provides the missing context for `HH523`. Its brief paper remark
(`Underpart darker than M. hylomyoides`) makes full sense only alongside these
notes. In NAHPU, both records live in one project: the entry is dated and names
Line 3, and `HH523` links to the `TJHH-L3` event, so anyone reading one can
find the other. On paper, `HH523`'s sheet had no locality and the journal sat in
a separate notebook.

{% aside type="note" title="What about the mist net?" %}
The 18 September entry mentions pulling a mist net at Pasir Putih. Mist nets on
this expedition were used for both bats and birds: NAHPU team member Ada
Chornelia cataloged the bats, and other team members cataloged the birds. We
excluded both here to keep these tutorials focused on Heru's terrestrial
small-mammal catalog.
{% /aside %}

## What you gained over paper

- The journal reads clearly with every fact intact and the original scan
  attached.
- Each entry is dated and linked to both its writer and the camp where it was
  written.
- Specimen records and journal entries cross-reference naturally—such as HH 436
  and the 12 September squirrel, or HH 523 and the possible new rat on 18
  September.

